<p>
  Quieto Skills turn the
  <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@quieto/tokens" rel="noopener">Quieto Tokens</a>
  CLI's commands into guided conversations in Claude Code. Each one asks for what
  it needs, runs the command, and explains what changed.
</p>

<h2 id="install">Install</h2>

<p>The skills drive the Quieto Tokens CLI, so the project needs it first:</p>

<pre><code>npm install @quieto/tokens</code></pre>

<p>
  Then install the skills. They are copied into <code>.claude/skills/</code> in
  the current directory, or in the directory you name:
</p>

<pre><code>npx @quieto/skills install
npx @quieto/skills install path/to/project</code></pre>

<h2 id="skills">Skills</h2>

<div class="table-scroll">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Command</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for skill in ai.skills %}
        <tr><td><code>/{{ skill.name }}</code></td><td>{{ skill.what }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<h2 id="use">Use them</h2>

<p>
  Run one by its command, such as <code>/design-token-init</code>, or describe
  the task (“audit my tokens for contrast problems”) and Claude Code loads the
  skill that fits.
</p>

<p>
  For an assistant other than Claude Code, the
  <a href="/ai/mcp/">MCP server</a> offers the same generation and checks as
  tools.
</p>
